h2 class=3D"corner-header">=0D=0A                              <img src=3D"=
https://assets.codepen.io/t-1/chriscorner.png" style=3D"width: 65px; border=
-radius: 4px;" /> Chris=E2=80=99 Corner=0D=0A                            </=
h2>=0D=0A                            <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection=
 of web design and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris C=
oyier.</p>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                  =
        <p>I=E2=80=99ve been doing computers for a good minute, but the <st=
rong>Scroll Lock</strong> key still predates me. There may have been a day =
when I could have explained what it does, but that knowledge had evaporated=
 until I read Marcin Wichary=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2=
.com/f/a/yuB0mLRsL9CMa1_Dv1x3AA~~/AABEfhA~/gMPKJMaFT8YLuSglp4oVHI5FlMx6Wc3=
O5KlKJNz8CGIxjx7t7UW5aauls_S4jxAGbLqFVRKPzjEYysHOSkUveuV0qxCR5iCgPauxtws7MN=
tho7f4cN15_Rdet35r8kasMuRehiisKjo2YnEvJxt4zHmuuoiSwVzgFeNPhh70VDw~">Lock Sc=
roll With a Vengeance</a>. It=E2=80=99s fascinating that the technology tha=
t deprecates the Scroll Lock key is=E2=80=A6 <em>the mouse</em> (and scroll=
bars and trackpads). Stuff that=E2=80=99s <em>been around</em>. I=E2=80=99m=
 very sure Excel nerds still <s>use</s> love it.</p>=0A<p>If I must fingers=
plain a fairly visual concept (I must): Scroll Lock, when enabled, keeps th=
e visually selected thing <em>right where it was</em> as you scroll, rather=
 than requiring the selected thing to be butted up against an edge to trigg=
er scrolling movement as it pushes beyond that edge. I think that was a per=
fect sentence, but your call. Go look at Marcin=E2=80=99s post to see how t=
his effect has actually made its way into modern interfaces, without the ke=
y=E2=80=99s opt-in. My take is that if you=E2=80=99re going to do this, the=
 selected item should be very centered so you can see other items in all di=
rections around it. The examples from HBO Max and YouTube just forced the l=
ocked item to the other edge. I=E2=80=99d bet a donut it=E2=80=99s so users=
 =E2=80=9Csee more sooner=E2=80=9D.</p>=0A<p>Welp, we=E2=80=99re getting a =
smidge reflective about design, here=E2=80=99s a zinger from Barry Prenderg=
ast: <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/dqx37xKpU4-5tPRYE8pg7w~~/=
AABEfhA~/p1gv4-AH6ukM0VJnVmpCxWv1phJd16qaAK133vCwcDhFQW7-l4bVpYqod0qlBHM6i7=
rYs4wTkOUcA9P4Jc8atpgGB210JhB305OpK9h0bE5uU-ZZsEbaY79-htfM8U-qRXerZYpHbIdD0=
aOMRGn6dA~~">The worst designer I=E2=80=99ve ever worked with was also the =
most productive</a>. It=E2=80=99s exactly what you think it is:</p>=0A<bloc=
kquote><p>This ambitious young designer pumped out more screens in a day th=
an more-experienced designers would in a week. Leadership adored them. They=
 saw the speed, the volume, the =E2=80=98beast mode=E2=80=99 energy =E2=80=
=94 and mistook it for value creation.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>It didn=E2=80=
=99t go well. The lesson=3F</p>=0A<blockquote><p>Good designers slow down w=
hen it matters. To clarify the problem. To align with their team. To create=
 outcomes that matter.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>I can feel how really talented=
 designers at big orgs are, <em>especially</em> feeling the AI anxiety. Whi=
ppin=E2=80=99 out screens ain=E2=80=99t it, which is what AI (and, apparent=
ly, particularly energetic and misguided junior devs) are good at. Real des=
ign has always been a much more nuanced affair. Jem Gold hits it nicely in =
<a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/nbvg44wtwpa1yfGDa27wdA~~/AABEf=
hA~/gcycSYU7zHSduM8T8XazYYJKtt_eUBkkXQhA_ryhFBczCICeLV3XzWfNt4lIpOUVUOkDTx1=
-tuuz90Ojw5UvGfBXRQazrBXEHsx9JlnkkWsNtPCjtnxXxJP5sTUQmIfE4ojhHBD-agw2k8_n-v=
THC1khB_L0gurGIJ2b2f3N0B4~">Design Is How It Tastes</a> with a banger subhe=
ad:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>If our design specs only tell models what the inte=
rface is made of, they have already forgotten what the interface is for</p>=
</blockquote>=0A<p>I admit I=E2=80=99m very impressed at what coding agents=
 can do these days, but I remain skeptical that AI can really understand th=
e human experience, a prereq for great design. At the moment, AI is headed =
for the more rote instructions-following output:</p>=0A<p>A few months ago,=
 Google Labs released <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/uqJ-U0qi=
P7ll9McOkN2sZg~~/AABEfhA~/LiE2K9oKWC7P1YF1_q7i7Jjhabv45IDwKS4hqXyWcL-CXqs3-=
6G3qa-WETPM3CYKurrcEM-ChLLv6nAmFoZxHA2-pL33wzTLKyekvFMlEucMn1GonKM4_Gpt2qyn=
VLq7-qrpbNceApca4nyX0HQAu29jJAo5AY6eS1oYu_4sqCGMHQU0Y6RiaIWvbMO9ul-8">DESIG=
N.md</a>. The idea is sound: codify your design ethos in a single markdown =
file so that coding agents can implement it consistently. Write it once, sh=
ip it everywhere. Portability matters because interfaces are increasingly b=
uilt by or with agents.</p>=0A<p>But read a typical DESIGN.md and you will =
find language like this:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>Use 8px radii. Use a neutral =
palette with a pink accent. Buttons should have primary and secondary state=
s. Cards should have subtle borders.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>Useful, but thin=
. It describes the produced object. It does not preserve the intended enco=
unter.</p>=0A<p>I used the word =E2=80=9Cgreat=E2=80=9D design above. A wor=
thy distinction. Certainly AI can poop out <em>generic but decent</em> desi=
gn. I see live design work powering successful companies regularly that wou=
ld probably benefit from letting an AI loose on their pages. Bringing up th=
e average bar is probably a good thing for the web. But <em>great</em> desi=
gn remains a human endeavor. CodePen Alum Jake Albaugh opens his <a href=3D=
"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/FF7dRjeK-vGLkPznncB9_Q~~/AABEfhA~/a7ViF6=
A8CrR973hlHx8ddZz3mmZSxD3pqqiP7dD_lkOtQtOte89QUDoG1PcPGICFZdLMsAJ6m9_DsADI_=
NlJdl4FFwo78lr2Vnd654L7H2BUAE074HLnJNRkRyQmYt2HXW2l017mfoWbRyRhDP8wWg~~">De=
sign is the work</a> article with:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>We=E2=80=99re in a =
moment where it has never been cheaper or faster to build something convinc=
ing.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>There is all this stuff we know that is absolute=
ly vital to what we build. Jake lists a small handful of that stuff:</p>=0A=
<blockquote><p>AI doesn=E2=80=99t know your constraints. It doesn=E2=80=99t=
 know your strategy. It doesn=E2=80=99t know what moment in the market you=
=E2=80=99re in, what your team is trying to prove, or what your customers a=
ctually need versus what they=E2=80=99ve said they want. The expectation =
=E2=80=94 the definition of what good looks like =E2=80=94 is something onl=
y you can provide.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>Blech enough AI for one Chris=E2=
=80=99 Corner.</p>=0A<p>How about a nice little design lesson from Jono Hey=
:</p>=0A<img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.c=
om/cdn-cgi/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/sketc=
h_jono.png" alt=3D"An illustrated 'sketchplanation' on front-loading keywor=
ds when writing for the web. The example is a list of conference features. =
The initial list of features reads: sessions for networking, all breaks wil=
l have coffee available for free, open workshops, the hotel has direct acce=
ss to the beach. The updated list reads: networking sessions, free coffee a=
t all breaks, workshops open to all, beach access from the hotel. Keywords =
up front!" width=3D"450"/>=0A<p>Writing, you say=3F Word choice is absolute=
ly a part of design. Great design, anyway.</p>=0A<p>I=E2=80=99ll leave you =
with <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/jn2CAfiE4ENPxoxqVa84Cg~~/=
AABEfhA~/dIVGqtQKR6RZtEpoIVSK2Xxh0jrEF9UhdibSpwXVo-eexzWfdmx2tSVPjIw8nX1BPB=
VOeH-bWZmwijzqZKrNxDF12owCy1qm4JlkwvOhRj1IL-Qs6y3sIxD8SIJLc8Qu">Design. Reg=
ret. <em>Confess</em>.</a> =E2=80=94 a site for all you designers out there=
 to share your profession disasters, then commisurate through reading other=
s.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>I once copied a logo from Logos That Slap for some =
concept sketch and it got approved.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>lol I mean AI cop=
ies, but humans <em>copy</em>.</p>=0D=0A                          =0D=0A   =
